==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|la|constitutum}}, past participle of [[constituo|constituere]]<!--which?? please fix this link-->. Constructed from the prefix {{m|la|con-}} and {{m|la|statuere||to place, set}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈkɒnstɪtjuːt/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈkɑnstɪt(j)uːt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-uk-constitute.ogg|Audio (UK)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[set up]]; to [[establish]]; to [[enact]].
#* {{RQ:Taylor HL}}
#*: Laws appointed and '''constituted''' by lawful authority.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[make up]]; to [[compose]]; to [[form]].
#* {{RQ:Johnson Cowley}}
#*: Truth and reason '''constitute''' that intellectual gold that defies destruction.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[appoint]], depute, or elect to an office; to make and empower.
#* {{RQ:Wordsworth The Excursion|4}}
#*: Me didst Thou '''constitute''' a priest of thine.

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|set up}} {{l|en|establish}}, {{l|en|enact}}
* {{sense|make up}} {{l|en|make up}}, {{l|en|compose}}, {{l|en|form}}; see also [[Thesaurus:compose]]
* {{sense|appoint}}

